//! The product manifest: what a product's CI publishes and what the
//! deployer installs.
//!
//! A manifest describes one release of one product as a template. Every
//! account-specific value is a placeholder from a closed set, and a
//! deployer renders the placeholders from an instance's own state before
//! it touches an account. Each product emits its manifest from its own
//! repository (cachet's `cachet-deploy` crate is the first), so this crate
//! knows no product: it knows the shape, the grammar, and the signature.
//!
//! Three things live here and nothing else. [`schema`] is the types and
//! their validation. [`placeholder`] is the grammar of `$` tokens and the
//! two renderers, one that refuses secrets and one that carries them.
//! [`signature`] verifies the ed25519 signature a product's CI puts over
//! the published bytes, which is what lets an agent in a customer's
//! account refuse a manifest a compromised control plane hands it.
//!
//! No I/O, no clock, no entropy. The agent, the control plane, and a
//! product's own tooling all call this crate, and a function of its
//! arguments answers the same everywhere.

#![forbid(unsafe_code)]

pub mod placeholder;
pub mod schema;
pub mod signature;

pub use schema::{Manifest, SchemaError, Violation};

/// The schema version this crate reads and writes. The placeholder set is
/// closed, and this number guards a product's emitter and the deployer's
/// renderer against drifting apart: a manifest naming another version is
/// refused before anything reads its fields.
pub const MANIFEST_VERSION: u32 = 1;

